= Interoperability =

BITwiki should exchange knowledge with external systems without pretending that differently designed ontologies mean the same thing merely because labels look similar.

V1 framed this as an '''Ontology Alignment Matrix'''. V2 preserves the core alignment discipline while separating it from unverified historical mappings.

Interoperability also applies inside the BITwiki ecosystem: [[BIThub]]/Discourse should consume canonical BITwiki state without becoming a second semantic authority.

== Why align ==

Interoperability can enable:

* import and enrichment from external knowledge graphs;
* export to common structured vocabularies;
* cross-system identifiers;
* query translation;
* source/provenance linking;
* comparison and verification across independent knowledge systems;
* BIThub/agent interfaces over canonical BITwiki entities and relationships;
* workflow round-trips from discussion/research into durable reviewed knowledge.

Alignment is useful only when semantic loss is understood.

== Mapping relations ==

A mapping should state how meanings relate.

=== Exact match ===
The two terms have materially the same intended meaning and scope for the current use.

Exact match should be rare and justified rather than assumed from identical labels.

=== Narrower match ===
The BITwiki concept is more specific than the external concept, or vice versa. Direction must be explicit.

=== Broader match ===
One concept covers a wider semantic range.

=== Close match ===
The concepts are substantially similar but differ enough that substitution may lose meaning.

=== Related match ===
The concepts are connected but are not equivalent enough for type/property substitution.

== Preserve context ==

When exporting/importing, do not silently discard:

* epistemic standing;
* provenance;
* uncertainty;
* source lineage;
* entity-type distinctions;
* relationship semantics;
* lifecycle state;
* V2-specific governance/Book Matter meaning where relevant.

If the target system has no equivalent field, explicitly identify the loss or use a documented extension mechanism.

== Bidirectional mapping is not guaranteed ==

A clean BITwiki→external mapping does not imply a lossless external→BITwiki mapping.

Example:

<pre>
BITwiki: specific evidentiary relationship
        ↓
external: generic relatedTo
</pre>

Once exported only as <code>relatedTo</code>, the original semantic strength cannot be reconstructed unless additional metadata travels with it.

== BIThub / Discourse interoperability ==

BIThub is not an external ontology in the ordinary sense, but it is a distinct application/runtime and therefore needs an explicit interface contract with MediaWiki.

The authority model is:

<pre>
BIThub / Discourse
interaction · discussion · workflow · user/agent context
        ↕
authenticated API / plugin / service / agent bridge
        ↕
BITwiki / MediaWiki
canonical pages · SMW graph · Cargo records · revisions
        ↓
Scribunto/Lua deterministic projections
</pre>

A BIThub integration may render a BITwiki entity card, relationship panel, graph neighborhood, comparison, knowledge-health diagnostic, or contribution task. The projection should be derived from canonical wiki state rather than copied into an independent Discourse-side semantic database unless a cache/index has an explicit synchronization contract.

Likewise, a BIThub conversation, agent result, or workflow transition should not silently mutate canonical BITwiki state. Writes require an authenticated integration path and must preserve author/process identity, provenance, review state, and the relevant page/Cargo/SMW contract.

Lua is not the network bridge. Scribunto executes inside MediaWiki and should produce deterministic wiki-resident transformations; cross-service transport belongs to explicit integration infrastructure.

== Interface-neutral projections ==

The Lua/compiler layer should prefer normalized view models when the same knowledge needs multiple presentations.

<pre>
SMW/Cargo/page state
        ↓
normalized BITwiki view model
        ├── MediaWiki HTML/wikitext
        ├── BIThub-native card/workflow
        ├── graph visualization
        └── agent/machine context
</pre>

This is interoperability without duplicating truth: presentation changes by consumer while canonical semantic state remains shared.

== External systems ==

V1 explored alignments with:

* Wikidata;
* Schema.org;
* Dublin Core;
* FOAF;
* RDF/RDFS/OWL-style vocabularies.

These remain natural interoperability targets.

Specific mappings must be checked against the current external vocabulary at implementation time. Do not copy historical Q-IDs/property assumptions simply because they appear in V1 documentation.

== BITwiki-specific semantics ==

Some BITwiki dimensions may have no direct external equivalent:

* epistemic standing;
* confidence rationale;
* provenance transformations;
* Book Matter anatomy;
* specialized relationship semantics;
* compiler diagnostics and derived display state.

The correct response is not to pretend equivalence. Preserve them as extensions, sidecar metadata, or explicitly documented unmapped fields.

== Import protocol ==

A safe import is not "copy external facts into BITwiki."

<pre>
identify source object + license/provenance
→ map external type/relations by meaning
→ preserve external identifier
→ import only supported fields
→ mark mapping relation + semantic losses
→ assign BITwiki epistemic metadata from evidence, not from source prestige
→ review surprising/high-impact claims
</pre>

Never assign <code>Established</code>, a numeric confidence, or any other epistemic standing solely because the source is Wikidata, Schema.org, Wikipedia, a journal, BIThub, or another respected system.

== Export protocol ==

<pre>
select exportable knowledge
→ map V2 entity/property semantics
→ identify unmapped V2 dimensions
→ serialize into target vocabulary/interface
→ retain provenance + identifiers
→ validate round-trip where practical
→ document lossy mappings
</pre>

== Mapping provenance ==

Mappings themselves are claims and should carry:

* source/target vocabulary or interface version;
* mapping author/process;
* mapping type;
* rationale;
* date reviewed;
* known semantic loss;
* external identifiers.

A stale mapping can be worse than no mapping because it appears machine-authoritative.

== V1 lineage ==

Primary V1 source: [[BITwiki Ontology Alignment Matrix]], plus [[Ontology Alignment Matrix.REVISED]] and [[Ontology Alignment Specification]].

V1's broad principles—semantic equivalence by meaning, context preservation, bidirectionality, documented loss—are retained. Specific historical mappings/default import rules remain in <code>archive-v1/</code> until individually verified.

